\lessonlabel{1}
\section{Sources of Law}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define the term \term{statute} and explain how statutes are created;
  \item describe what is meant by \term{common law} and give an example of a common law principle;
  \item identify the role of \term{regulations} and how they differ from statutes;
  \item outline the steps courts take when they apply the law to a case.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Statute}

A \term{statute} is a law that has been formally enacted by a legislative body such as a parliament or congress. The process usually begins with a proposal called a bill. The bill is debated, possibly amended, and then voted on. If a majority approves, the bill receives \term{royal assent} or a similar formal approval and becomes an \term{act of Parliament} or \term{statute}. 

Statutes are written in a structured format. They contain sections, subsections and schedules that organise the content. For example, a criminal code might have a section that defines theft, a subsection that lists the elements of the offence, and a schedule that sets out the maximum penalties. The language of a statute is intended to be clear and precise, because courts will interpret the wording when disputes arise.

Statutes can be \term{primary legislation} (the act itself) or \term{secondary legislation} (rules made under the authority of the act). Secondary legislation includes \term{regulations}, \term{orders} and \term{by laws}. These are often used to fill in technical details that the primary legislation leaves open.

\begin{example}
The Health Protection Act (a statute) gives the Minister of Health the power to make regulations about food safety. The Act itself states that ``the Minister may prescribe standards for food handling.'' The subsequent Food Safety Regulations (secondary legislation) specify the exact temperature at which meat must be stored, the required labelling format, and the inspection schedule for restaurants.
\end{example}

\subsection{Common Law}

\term{Common law} is law that develops from judicial decisions rather than from statutes. It originates in the English legal system and has been adopted, with variations, in many common law jurisdictions such as the United States, Canada, Australia and India. When a court decides a case, it writes a \term{judgment} that explains the reasoning and the legal rule applied. That rule becomes part of the common law and can be cited in later cases.

A key feature of common law is the doctrine of \term{stare decisis}, which means ``to stand by things decided.'' Under this doctrine, lower courts must follow the legal principles established by higher courts in the same jurisdiction. This creates consistency and predictability. However, higher courts may overrule earlier decisions if they consider them outdated or incorrect.

Common law principles often fill gaps left by statutes. For instance, the tort of \term{negligence} is largely a common law concept. The classic elements---duty of care, breach of that duty, causation and damage---were articulated by courts over many decades, long before any statute codified them.

\begin{example}
In the case of Donoghue v Stevenson, the House of Lords held that a manufacturer owes a duty of care to the ultimate consumer of its product. The judgment established the modern concept of negligence and is still cited in countless cases involving personal injury, product liability and professional standards.
\end{example}

\subsection{Regulations}

\term{Regulations} are rules made by an executive authority---often a government department or agency---under powers granted by a statute. They are sometimes called \term{delegated legislation} because the legislature delegates authority to create detailed rules. Regulations have the force of law, but they are generally easier to amend than statutes, allowing the law to adapt to technical or scientific developments.

Regulations differ from statutes in several ways. First, they are usually more specific and technical. Second, the process for making them is shorter: a draft regulation may be published for public comment, then signed by the responsible minister. Third, the scope of a regulation is limited to the powers given by the parent statute; a regulation cannot create new offences that the statute does not authorize.

Because regulations are detailed, they are often the primary source of compliance requirements for businesses. For example, environmental regulations may set emission limits, reporting procedures and penalties for non compliance, all derived from an overarching Environmental Protection Act.

\subsection{How Courts Apply the Law}

When a court receives a case, it follows a systematic method to apply the law:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Identify the relevant facts.} The judge examines the evidence and determines the material facts that will affect the legal analysis.
  \item \textbf{Determine the applicable law.} The court looks for statutes, regulations and relevant common law precedents that govern the issues identified.
  \item \textbf{Interpret the law.} If the wording of a statute or regulation is ambiguous, the court uses interpretive tools such as the plain meaning rule, the purposive approach, or the use of legislative history.
  \item \textbf{Apply the law to the facts.} The judge matches the legal rules to the factual situation, deciding whether the elements of a claim are satisfied or whether a defence applies.
  \item \textbf{Reach a decision.} The court issues a judgment that may include orders, damages, or other remedies. The reasoning is recorded so that future courts can follow or distinguish the case.
\end{enumerate}

The interaction between statutes, regulations and common law is dynamic. A statute may expressly override a common law rule, but where the statute is silent, the court will rely on common law. Regulations must be read in light of the enabling statute; if a regulation exceeds the authority granted, a court may declare it invalid.

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} When faced with a legal problem, ask: (1) Is there a statute that directly addresses the issue? (2) If not, does a regulation issued under a relevant statute fill the gap? (3) If neither exists, is there a common law principle that applies? Follow the steps of fact finding, legal identification, interpretation, application and decision.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Statute & A law enacted by a legislative body; also called primary legislation. \\
Common law & Law developed from judicial decisions and the doctrine of stare decisis. \\
Regulation & Detailed rule made by an executive authority under powers granted by a statute. \\
Stare decisis & The principle that courts must follow earlier judicial decisions of higher courts. \\
Delegated legislation & Law created by an agency or minister under authority given by a statute. \\
Interpretation & The process by which courts give meaning to the words of statutes and regulations. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Contracts}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define what an offer is and identify its essential features;
  \item explain how acceptance creates a binding agreement;
  \item describe the role of consideration in a contract;
  \item assess whether parties intended to create legal relations.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Offer}

An \term{offer} is a clear, definite proposal made by one party (the offeror) to another (the offeree) with the intention that it become binding if the offeree agrees. The essential elements are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Clear terms.} The subject matter, price, quantity and any other essential terms must be specified.
  \item \term{Communication.} The offer must be communicated to the offeree, either directly or by a reliable channel.
  \item \term{Intent.} The offeror must intend to be bound by the proposal if the offeree accepts.
\end{itemize}

An invitation to treat, such as a shop display or an advertisement, is not an offer because it lacks the intention to be bound. It merely invites others to make offers.

\begin{example}
A seller posts a sign: ``Bicycles for sale -- \$150 each.'' This is an invitation to treat. When a customer says, ``I will buy the red bike for \$150,'' the customer is making an offer. The seller can accept, reject, or propose a different price.
\end{example}

\subsection{Acceptance}

\term{Acceptance} is the unqualified agreement to the terms of the offer. It must be:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Unconditional.} Any change to the terms creates a counter offer, not acceptance.
  \item \term{Communicated.} Acceptance must be communicated to the offeror, unless the offer provides a method that does not require communication (e.g., performance of a unilateral contract).
  \item \term{Made within the time limit.} If the offer specifies a deadline, acceptance after that deadline is ineffective.
\end{itemize}

The ``mailbox rule'' states that acceptance is effective when it is posted, not when it is received, provided the post is a reasonable method of communication.

\begin{example}
Anna emails a contractor: ``I will pay \$2,000 for a new kitchen cabinet by 1 May.'' The contractor replies, ``I accept.'' The contract is formed at the moment the email is sent, because the acceptance was communicated unconditionally and within the stated time.
\end{example}

\subsection{Consideration}

\term{Consideration} is the value exchanged between the parties. It can be a promise, an act, or forbearance, but it must have some legal value. The basic rule is that each party must give something of value; a contract without consideration is generally void.

Key points:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Sufficient but not adequate.} The law does not require the consideration to be equal in value, only that it is recognised as having some value.
  \item \term{Past consideration.} Something already performed before the promise is made does not count as consideration.
  \item \term{Promissory estoppel.} In limited cases, a promise may be enforced even without consideration if the promisee has relied on it to their detriment.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
Tom promises to give his neighbour \$500 if the neighbour paints Tom's fence. The neighbour's act of painting is consideration; Tom's promise to pay is consideration. If Tom later refuses to pay, the neighbour can enforce the contract because both sides exchanged something of value.
\end{example}

\subsection{Intention to Create Legal Relations}

For a contract to be enforceable, the parties must intend that their agreement be legally binding. The law distinguishes between:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Commercial agreements.} The presumption is that parties intend legal relations unless evidence shows otherwise.
  \item \term{Domestic or social agreements.} The presumption is that parties do not intend legal relations, unless the context demonstrates a contrary intention.
\end{itemize}

Courts look at the language used, the surrounding circumstances, and any written terms that expressly state the parties' intention.

\begin{example}
A couple agrees that one will mow the lawn each week in exchange for the other cooking dinner. In a domestic setting, the court is likely to view this as a social arrangement without legal intention, unless the parties have written a formal agreement stating otherwise.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Determine whether a contract exists by checking four questions: (1) Is there a clear offer? (2) Has the offeree unconditionally accepted? (3) Is there consideration moving in both directions? (4) Did the parties intend the agreement to be legally enforceable? If the answer to all four is yes, a contract is formed.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Offer & A definite proposal that becomes binding if accepted. \\
Acceptance & Unconditional agreement to the terms of an offer, communicated within the required time. \\
Consideration & Something of legal value exchanged between parties; must be present for a contract to be enforceable. \\
Intention to create legal relations & The parties' intention that their agreement be enforceable by law; presumed in commercial deals, not in domestic ones. \\
Invitation to treat & An invitation for others to make offers; not an offer itself. \\
Counter offer & A response that changes the terms of the original offer, thereby rejecting it. \\
Promissory estoppel & An exception allowing enforcement of a promise without consideration when reliance is detrimental. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{3}
\section{Breach and Remedies}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item identify the main types of breach of contract;
  \item explain how damages are calculated and what limits apply;
  \item describe when a court will order specific performance;
  \item distinguish between prohibitory and mandatory injunctions and state the requirements for each.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Types of breach}

A breach occurs when a party fails to perform a contractual duty. The law recognises three principal categories.

\term{Minor breach} (or partial breach) is a failure to perform a non essential term. The contract remains alive, and the innocent party may claim damages but cannot terminate the agreement. For example, a supplier delivers 95 percent of the agreed quantity on time; the shortfall is a minor breach.

\term{Material breach} is a failure to perform a essential term that goes to the heart of the contract. The innocent party may treat the contract as terminated and may also claim damages. An example is a builder who abandons a construction project before completing the structure.

\term{Fundamental breach} (also called repudiatory breach) is a deliberate or serious refusal to perform that shows the breaching party no longer intends to be bound. The innocent party can immediately rescind the contract and sue for damages. A classic case is a seller who sells a car that does not exist.

The distinction matters because it determines the remedies that are available. Minor breaches give rise only to monetary compensation, whereas material and fundamental breaches open the door to termination, specific performance, or injunctions.

\begin{example}
A software developer contracts to deliver a custom application by 1 June. On 15 May the developer informs the client that the project will be delayed by three months. The delay defeats the purpose of the contract, which was to support a product launch on 1 July. This is a material breach, allowing the client to terminate the contract and claim damages for the lost launch opportunity.
\end{example}

\subsection{Damages}

Damages are a monetary award intended to put the innocent party in the position they would have been in had the contract been performed. The law uses several principles to calculate the amount.

\term{Compensatory damages} cover the loss directly caused by the breach. The formula is usually \[ \text{Loss} = \text{Benefit of the bargain} - \text{Cost of performance} \] where the benefit of the bargain is the value the innocent party expected to receive.

\term{Consequential damages} (or special damages) cover losses that flow indirectly from the breach, provided they were reasonably foreseeable at the time of contracting. For instance, a retailer who cannot sell a product because the supplier failed to deliver may claim loss of profit as consequential damages.

\term{Nominal damages} are a small sum awarded when a breach is proven but the innocent party cannot show actual loss. They serve to acknowledge the legal right that was violated.

\term{Punitive damages} are rarely awarded in contract law because the purpose of contract damages is compensation, not punishment. Some jurisdictions allow them only where the breach involves fraud or malicious conduct.

Two important limits apply:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Mitigation} requires the innocent party to take reasonable steps to reduce their loss. Failure to mitigate can reduce the damages awarded.
  \item \term{Remoteness} limits damages to losses that were reasonably foreseeable as a probable result of the breach at the time the contract was formed.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A concert promoter contracts with a band for a performance worth \$50,000. The band cancels two weeks before the event. The promoter can claim \$50,000 as the benefit of the bargain, plus \$10,000 in lost ticket sales that were reasonably foreseeable, minus any costs saved by not having to pay the venue. If the promoter could have booked a replacement act for \$20,000, the court may reduce the award by that amount as part of mitigation.
\end{example}

\subsection{Specific performance}

Specific performance is an equitable remedy that orders the breaching party to carry out their contractual obligations. It is used when damages are inadequate to compensate the innocent party.

The court will grant specific performance only if:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the subject matter is unique or has special value (e.g., real estate, rare artwork);
  \item the contract is clear and enforceable;
  \item the order is practicable to supervise;
  \item the parties are of equal bargaining power and there is no evidence of undue hardship.
\end{itemize}

Specific performance is discretionary. Even where the subject is unique, the court may refuse if it would cause undue hardship or if monetary damages can adequately compensate.

\begin{example}
A buyer contracts to purchase a historic townhouse that is the only remaining example of a particular architectural style in the city. The seller later refuses to complete the sale. Because the property is unique, the court orders specific performance, compelling the seller to transfer title to the buyer.
\end{example}

\subsection{Injunctions}

An injunction is a court order that either restrains a party from doing something (prohibitory injunction) or requires them to do something (mandatory injunction). Injunctions are also equitable remedies and are granted only when damages are insufficient.

\term{Prohibitory injunction} prevents a future breach. The plaintiff must show a serious threat of irreparable harm, that damages would not compensate, and that the balance of convenience favours the injunction. A classic case is a former employee who signed a non compete clause; the employer may seek a prohibitory injunction to stop the employee from joining a competitor.

\term{Mandatory injunction} compels affirmative action. It is less common because it interferes more with the defendant's freedom. The court will order a mandatory injunction when the plaintiff can demonstrate that the defendant's conduct has caused ongoing damage that cannot be measured in money. For example, a court may order a builder to remove an illegally constructed extension that encroaches on a neighbour's land.

Key requirements for any injunction are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Clear right} -- the plaintiff must have a legally recognised right;
  \item \term{Irreparable harm} -- the injury cannot be adequately remedied by damages;
  \item \term{Balance of convenience} -- the harm to the plaintiff if the injunction is denied must outweigh the harm to the defendant if it is granted.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A pharmaceutical company holds a patent on a life saving drug. A competitor begins manufacturing a generic copy that infringes the patent. The patent holder seeks a prohibitory injunction. The court finds that the competitor's sales would cause irreparable loss of market share and that damages would be difficult to calculate, so it grants an injunction preventing further production of the generic drug.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} When a breach occurs, ask: Is the loss purely monetary? If yes, damages are appropriate. If the subject is unique, consider specific performance. If the breach threatens ongoing or irreparable harm, evaluate whether a prohibitory or mandatory injunction is required, remembering the need for a clear right, irreparable harm, and a favourable balance of convenience.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Minor breach & Failure to perform a non essential term; contract remains in force. \\
Material breach & Failure to perform an essential term; allows termination and damages. \\
Fundamental breach & Serious refusal to perform, showing intent to abandon the contract; permits rescission. \\
Compensatory damages & Monetary award that puts the innocent party in the position they would have been in. \\
Consequential damages & Losses that flow indirectly from the breach, if foreseeable. \\
Mitigation & Duty of the innocent party to reduce their loss where reasonably possible. \\
Remoteness & Limitation on damages to losses that were foreseeable at contract formation. \\
Specific performance & Court order requiring the breaching party to fulfil the contract, used for unique subject matter. \\
Prohibitory injunction & Order preventing a party from doing something that would cause irreparable harm. \\
Mandatory injunction & Order requiring a party to take action to prevent ongoing damage. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{4}
\section{Agency}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define what an agency is in legal and business contexts;
  \item describe the steps required to create a valid agency;
  \item list the main duties of agents toward their principals;
  \item list the main duties of principals toward their agents;
  \item recognise situations where an agency relationship may be implied or apparent.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{What is an agency?}

An \term{agency} is a relationship in which one person, the \term{agent}, is authorised to act on behalf of another person, the \term{principal}, so that the legal effects of the agent's actions bind the principal. The authority may be express, implied or apparent. An agency can arise in many settings: a real estate broker selling a house, a lawyer signing a contract for a client, or a student representative negotiating with university administration.

The key feature of an agency is that the principal remains ultimately responsible for the agent's lawful acts. If the agent exceeds authority, the principal may still be bound if the third party reasonably believes the agent had authority (apparent authority).

\subsection{Creating an agency}

Creating a valid agency requires three essential elements:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Consent.} Both principal and agent must agree to the relationship. Consent can be written, oral, or inferred from conduct.
  \item \term{Capacity.} Each party must have legal capacity to contract. A minor, a person lacking mental capacity, or an insolvent company may be unable to create an agency.
  \item \term{Authority.} The principal must intend to give the agent the power to act on the principal's behalf. Authority may be:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \term{Express authority} -- a clear statement, often in a written contract, specifying the scope of the agent's powers.
      \item \term{Implied authority} -- powers necessary to carry out the express authority, such as a manager's power to hire staff.
      \item \term{Apparent authority} -- created when the principal's words or conduct lead a third party to reasonably believe the agent is authorised.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

Formal agencies are usually documented in a contract called an \term{agency agreement}. The agreement sets out the scope of authority, duration, remuneration, and termination provisions. Even when no written contract exists, the relationship can be established by conduct, such as a shop owner allowing a clerk to accept payments on his behalf.

\begin{example}
A university hires a recruitment officer to enrol students for a semester. The university signs a written agreement giving the officer authority to sign enrolment forms, collect tuition fees, and issue student IDs. The officer's authority is express because it is stated in the contract. The officer also has implied authority to arrange campus tours, because such activities are necessary to enrol students. If the officer later signs a lease for a new office without written permission, the university may argue the officer lacked authority, unless a third party could reasonably assume the officer had apparent authority based on past conduct.
\end{example}

\subsection{Duties of the agent}

Agents owe several fiduciary duties to their principals:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Duty of loyalty.} The agent must act solely in the principal's best interests and avoid conflicts of interest. The agent may not profit from the agency without the principal's informed consent.
  \item \term{Duty of obedience.} The agent must follow lawful instructions given by the principal and stay within the scope of authority.
  \item \term{Duty of care and skill.} The agent must perform tasks with reasonable competence and diligence, using the level of skill expected of a professional in that field.
  \item \term{Duty to account.} The agent must keep accurate records of all transactions and provide the principal with a full accounting of money received, expenses incurred, and profits earned.
  \item \term{Duty of confidentiality.} The agent must not disclose the principal's confidential information obtained during the agency, unless authorised or required by law.
\end{itemize}

Failure to meet these duties can give the principal a right to sue for breach of fiduciary duty, to recover losses, or to terminate the agency.

\subsection{Duties of the principal}

Principals also have obligations toward their agents:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Payment.} The principal must pay the agreed remuneration, whether a salary, commission, or fee, on time and in the manner stipulated.
  \item \term{Reimbursement.} The principal must reimburse the agent for reasonable expenses incurred while acting on the principal's behalf, provided the expenses are authorised or necessary.
  \item \term{Indemnity.} The principal must protect the agent against liabilities that arise from acting within the scope of authority, including legal costs and damages.
  \item \term{Cooperation.} The principal must provide the information, documents, and resources the agent needs to perform the duties effectively.
\end{itemize}

If a principal breaches these duties, the agent may claim damages, seek restitution, or terminate the agency.

\subsection{Termination of an agency}

An agency may end in several ways:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Mutual agreement.} Both parties agree to end the relationship.
  \item \term{Performance.} The purpose of the agency is fulfilled, such as a sales agent completing a specific transaction.
  \item \term{Expiration.} The agency agreement specifies a fixed term that expires.
  \item \term{Revocation.} The principal may revoke authority, provided the revocation does not breach the contract or cause undue prejudice.
  \item \term{Renunciation.} The agent may resign, giving reasonable notice.
  \item \term{Death or incapacity.} The death or legal incapacity of either party generally ends the agency, unless the agreement provides otherwise.
  \item \term{Bankruptcy.} The bankruptcy of the principal or agent typically terminates the agency, subject to statutory rules.
\end{enumerate}

Termination does not relieve the principal of liability for acts performed before termination, nor does it relieve the agent of duties owed for past conduct.

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} To decide whether an agency exists, ask: (1) Did the principal give the agent authority to act on the principal's behalf? (2) Did the agent accept that authority? (3) Is there evidence of consent, capacity, and a lawful purpose? If the answers are yes, an agency relationship is likely.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Agency & Relationship where an agent acts on behalf of a principal. \\
Principal & The person who authorises another to act for them. \\
Agent & The person authorised to act for the principal. \\
Express authority & Authority given directly, usually in writing. \\
Implied authority & Authority inferred as necessary to carry out express authority. \\
Apparent authority & Authority that a third party reasonably believes exists based on the principal's conduct. \\
Duty of loyalty & Agent must act only in the principal's interests. \\
Duty of obedience & Agent must follow the principal's lawful instructions. \\
Duty of care and skill & Agent must perform tasks competently. \\
Duty to account & Agent must keep and provide accurate financial records. \\
Duty of confidentiality & Agent must protect the principal's private information. \\
Indemnity & Principal's obligation to cover the agent's liabilities incurred within authority. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
